4;124;124m5[0m[4B[2A[2C[?25h[?2026l[?2026h[21A[J[38;2;124;124;124mLet me optimize the approach. The brute force is too slow. Let me use a smarter [0m[38;2;124;124;124mmethod.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the column-based approach: we process columns left to right. For each [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcolumn, we know the multiset of values needed. We assign values to elements for [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthat column, subject to the non-increasing constraint (each element's value in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcolumn $i$ must be $\le$ its value in column $i-1$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a transfer matrix approach. The state is the multiset of values assigned[0m[38;2;124;124;124mso far to each element (or more precisely, the values in the previous column, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhich constrain the current column).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me think about it differently. Let me process column by column. At[0m[38;2;124;124;124mcolumn $i$, the state is the assignment of values to the $2n$ elements from the [0m[38;2;124;124;124mprevious column (column $i-1$), which constrains the current column. But the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstate space is huge.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA better approach: since elements are labeled but interchangeable (the count is [0m[38;2;124;124;124msymmetric), we can think of this as counting the number of $2n \times (n+1)$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmatrices with weakly decreasing rows and specified column content. This is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mequivalent to counting the number of ways to fill such a matrix.[0m

